{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and Prince of Galilee from 1174 to 1187. He was a member of the House of Toulouse and played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century.\n\n**Early Life and Reign**\nRaymond III was born around 1140 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152. Raymond III's early reign was marked by conflicts with the neighboring Muslim states and the Byzantine Empire.\n\n**Marriage and Family**\nRaymond III married Eschiva Princess of Galilee in 1174 and became the Prince of Galilee. The marriage strengthened Raymond's position in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and gave him control over the northern territories.\n\n*War with Saladin*\nIn 1174 Raymond III led a successful campaign against the forces of Saladin in the Battle of Ain al-Baqar. However Saladin eventually captured the city of Banias in 1164 and Raymond was forced to sign a truce. The truce was short-lived as Raymond III allied with the Byzantine Empire and launched a new campaign against Saladin in 1167.\n\n**Battle of Hattin and Aftermath**\nIn 1187 Raymond III led a contingent of troops from Tripoli and Galilee to support King Guy of Lusignan in the Battle of Hattin. The battle was a disaster for the Crusaders and Raymond III was forced to flee. Saladin captured the city of Tiberias and Raymond's wife Eschiva was taken prisoner. Raymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV.\n\n**Legacy**\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century. He was a skilled military leader and diplomat who maintained alliances with the Byzantine Empire and other Christian states. However his inability to defeat Saladin ultimately led to the downfall of the Crus